    year: 2006,
    bar_chair: 'Justice Angelina Sandoval-Gutierrez',
    question_text: 'State whether or not the following laws are constitutional. Explain briefly.\n\nA law prohibiting Chinese citizens from engaging in retail trade. 2%\n\nA law denying persons charged with crimes punishable by reclusion perpetua or death the right to bail. 2%\n\nA law fixing the terms of local elective officials, other than barangay officials, to 6 years. 2%\n\nA law changing the design of the Philippine flag. 2%\n\nA law creating a state corporation to exploit, develop, and utilize compressed natural gas. 2%',
    suggested_answer: '1. Constitutional. In Ichong v. Hernandez, the Supreme Court upheld the Retail Trade Nationalization Law, ruling that the State, in the exercise of its police power, can reserve certain areas of business for its own citizens to protect the national economy.\n\n2. Unconstitutional. Under the Bill of Rights, all persons, except those charged with offenses punishable by reclusion perpetua when evidence of guilt is strong, shall, before conviction, be bailable by sufficient sureties. A law that denies bail to all persons charged with such crimes, regardless of the strength of evidence, violates the Constitution.\n\n3. Unconstitutional. Section 8, Article X of the Constitution explicitly provides that the term of office of elective local officials, except barangay officials, shall be three years. A law extending this to six years would require a constitutional amendment.\n\n4. Constitutional. Section 1, Article XVI of the Constitution allows Congress to adopt a new name for the country, a new anthem, or a new design for the flag, which shall be truly reflective and symbolic of the ideals, history, and traditions of the people. Such a law, however, must be submitted to the people in a national referendum for ratification.\n\n5. Constitutional. Section 2, Article XII of the Constitution allows the State to directly undertake the exploration, development, and utilization of natural resources, or it may enter into co-production, joint venture, or production-sharing agreements with Filipino citizens or corporations.'
