    bar_chair: 'Justice Angelina Sandoval-Gutierrez',
    question_text: 'What is the function of the Senate Electoral Tribunal and the House of Representatives Electoral Tribunal? 2.5%\n\nWhat is the composition of each? 2.5%\n\nDifferentiate an election protest from an action for quo warranto. 2.5%\n\nWhat is a quasi-judicial body or agency? 2.5%',
    suggested_answer: '1. The Senate Electoral Tribunal (SET) and the House of Representatives Electoral Tribunal (HRET) are the sole judges of all contests relating to the election, returns, and qualifications of their respective members.\n\n2. Each Tribunal is composed of nine members: three are Justices of the Supreme Court designated by the Chief Justice, and the remaining six are members of the Senate or the House of Representatives, as the case may be, chosen on the basis of proportional representation from the political parties represented therein. The senior Justice in each Tribunal acts as its Chairman.\n\n3. An election protest is a contest between candidates for a particular office, involving a challenge to the election returns on grounds of fraud, terrorism, or irregularities. An action for quo warranto is a challenge to a candidate\'s title to the office on the ground of ineligibility or disloyalty to the Republic.\n\n4. A quasi-judicial body or agency is an administrative unit of the government, other than a court, which has the power and authority to adjudicate or determine the rights and obligations of parties in a particular matter within its jurisdiction, following a procedure that is judicial in nature (notice and hearing).'
