    year: 2006,
    bar_chair: 'Justice Angelina Sandoval-Gutierrez',
    question_text: 'a) What is the principal identifying feature of a presidential form of government? Explain. 2.5%\n\nb) What are the essential characteristics of a parliamentary form of government? 2.5%\n\nWhat Constitutional provisions institutionalize the principle of civilian supremacy? 2.5%\n\nDoes a Permit to Carry Firearm Outside Residence (PTCFOR) constitute a property right protected by the Constitution? 2.5%',
    suggested_answer: '1. The principal identifying feature of a presidential form of government is the separation of powers. The executive, legislative, and judicial branches are co-equal and independent of each other. The President is both the head of state and head of government and is elected independently of the legislature.\n\n2. In a parliamentary form of government, there is a fusion of executive and legislative powers. The executive (Prime Minister and Cabinet) is drawn from and is responsible to the legislature. The head of state (e.g., a Monarch or President) is usually a ceremonial figure, while the head of government (Prime Minister) wields actual power.\n\n3. Section 3, Article II of the Constitution states: "Civilian authority is, at all times, supreme over the military. The Armed Forces of the Philippines is the protector of the people and the State. Its goal is to secure the sovereignty of the State and the integrity of the national territory." Also, the President, a civilian, is the Commander-in-Chief of all armed forces.\n\n4. No. In Chavez v. Romulo (2004), the Supreme Court ruled that a permit to carry a firearm is not a property right but a mere statutory privilege. It is subject to the police power of the State and can be revoked or suspended for public safety reasons.'
