    bar_chair: 'Justice Angelina Sandoval-Gutierrez',
    question_text: 'Select the best answer and explain.\n\nAn accused\'s right against self-incrimination is violated in the following cases: 5%\n\nWhen he is ordered by the trial court to undergo a paraffin test to prove he is guilty of murder;\n\nWhen he is compelled to produce his bankbooks to be used as evidence against his father charged with plunder;\n\nWhen he is ordered to produce a sample of his handwriting to be used as evidence that he is the author of a letter wherein he agreed to kill the victim;\n\nWhen the president of a corporation is subpoenaed to produce certain documents as proofs he is guilty of illegal recruitment.\n\nThe legislature may abolish this body: 5%\n\nCommission on Appointments\n\nOmbudsman\n\nJudicial and Bar Council\n\nCourt of Tax Appeals\n\nCommission on Audit',
    suggested_answer: '1. The best answer is: "When he is ordered to produce a sample of his handwriting to be used as evidence that he is the author of a letter wherein he agreed to kill the victim." The right against self-incrimination protects against testimonial compulsion. While physical tests like paraffin tests or DNA tests are generally allowed as they involve the body as physical evidence, handwriting is considered testimonial because it involves the "intelligence" and "will" of the accused to produce a specific script for comparison.\n\n2. The best answer is: "Court of Tax Appeals." The Commission on Appointments, the Ombudsman, the Judicial and Bar Council, and the Commission on Audit are all constitutional bodies created by the 1987 Constitution itself. They cannot be abolished by a mere act of the legislature. The Court of Tax Appeals, however, is a statutory court created by law (R.A. No. 1125) and can be abolished or modified by Congress.'
