    question_text: 'Where is the seat of the International Court of Justice? 1%\n\nHow many are its members? 1 %\n\nWhat is the term of their office? 1 %\n\nWho is its incumbent president? 1 %\n\nWhat is his/her nationality? 1 %\n\nIn 1980, the United States filed with the International Court of Justice a complaint against Iran alleging that the latter is detaining American diplomats in violation of International Law. Explain how the International Court of Justice can acquire jurisdiction over these contending countries. 5%',
    suggested_answer: '1. The seat of the International Court of Justice (ICJ) is at the Peace Palace in The Hague, Netherlands.\n\n2. The ICJ is composed of 15 judges.\n\n3. The term of office for ICJ judges is nine years, and they may be re-elected.\n\n4. As of 2006 (the time of the exam), the President was Rosalyn Higgins.\n\n5. Rosalyn Higgins is British (United Kingdom).\n\n6. The ICJ acquires jurisdiction over states based on their consent. This consent can be expressed in several ways: (a) by a special agreement (compromis) to submit a specific dispute to the Court; (b) by a jurisdictional clause in a treaty; or (c) by a reciprocal declaration under the "Optional Clause" (Article 36, paragraph 2 of the ICJ Statute) where a state recognizes the Court\'s jurisdiction as compulsory in relation to any other state accepting the same obligation. In the 1980 case (United States v. Iran), jurisdiction was based on the 1961 Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations and the 1963 Vienna Convention on Consular Relations, to which both states were parties and which contained protocols for the settlement of disputes by the ICJ.'
