    bar_chair: 'Justice Angelina Sandoval-Gutierrez',
    question_text: 'How is state sovereignty defined in International Law? 2.5%\n\nIs state sovereignty absolute? 2.5%\n\nWhat is the principle of auto-limitation? 2.5%\n\nWhat is the relationship between reciprocity and the principle of auto-limitation? 2.5%',
    suggested_answer: '1. State sovereignty is the supreme power of a state to command and enforce obedience, the power to which, within the state, all persons and institutions are subject, and which is independent of any external control. In international law, it refers to the legal personality of a state as an independent entity with the right to exercise exclusive jurisdiction over its territory and people.\n\n2. No, state sovereignty is not absolute in the international sphere. It is limited by the rules of international law, treaties entered into by the state, and the sovereignty of other states. The concept of "sovereign equality" implies that each state\'s sovereignty is limited by the equal rights of others.\n\n3. The principle of auto-limitation (or self-limitation) is the doctrine where a state, by its own consent, imposes limits upon its own sovereignty by entering into treaties or accepting international obligations. It is based on the idea that the state is not subject to any external will except that which it has itself consented to.\n\n4. Reciprocity is often the basis or the motivation for auto-limitation. A state agrees to limit its sovereignty (auto-limitation) on the condition or expectation that other states will do the same (reciprocity). This mutual exchange of rights and obligations is fundamental to the stability and functioning of the international legal order.'
