    year: 2006,
    bar_chair: 'Justice Angelina Sandoval-Gutierrez',
    question_text: 'What is the purpose of labor legislation? 2.5%\n\nWhat is the concept of liberal approach in interpreting the Labor Code and its Implementing Rules and Regulations in favor of labor? 2.5%\n\nWhat property right is conferred upon an employee once there is an employer-employee relationship? Discuss briefly. 5%',
    suggested_answer: '1. The purpose of labor legislation is to provide protection to the worker, promote full employment, ensure equal work opportunities regardless of sex, race or creed, and regulate the relations between workers and employers. It is an exercise of the police power of the State to achieve social justice.\n\n2. The liberal approach is embodied in Article 4 of the Labor Code, which provides that all doubts in the implementation and interpretation of the provisions of the Code, including its implementing rules and regulations, shall be resolved in favor of labor. This is a constitutional mandate to protect the rights of workers who are often in a weaker bargaining position.\n\n3. The right to labor or employment is considered a property right within the meaning of the due process clause of the Constitution. Once an employer-employee relationship is established, the employee acquires a vested right to their position and cannot be deprived of it without just or authorized cause and after observance of procedural due process.'
