    suggested_answer: '1. A wage distortion exists when an increase in prescribed wage rates results in the elimination or severe contraction of intentional quantitative differences in wage or salary levels between and among employee groups in an establishment as to effectively obliterate the distinctions embodied in such wage structure based on skills, length of service, or other logical bases of differentiation.\n\n2. Wage distortions should be settled through the grievance machinery provided in the Collective Bargaining Agreement (CBA), and if unresolved, through voluntary arbitration. In establishments without a CBA, the employer and the employees shall endeavor to settle the dispute. If it remains unresolved, it shall be referred to the National Conciliation and Mediation Board (NCMB) for conciliation, and if that fails, to the NLRC.\n\n3. No. Under Article 124 of the Labor Code, wage distortion is not a strikeable issue. The law provides for a specific mandatory procedure for its resolution to prevent industrial unrest over issues that can be settled through technical or administrative means.'
